  "status": "approved",
  "title": "",
  "classification": {
    "primarySkillId": "solve-inverse-function-problems",
    "secondarySkillIds": [
      "verify-inverse-composition",
      "reflection-line-y-equals-x",
      "one-to-one-functions",
      "domain-range-inverse"
    ],
    "reasoningScore": 55,
    "difficulty": "Mastery",
    "difficultyReason": "Distinguishes inverse identities and their domains, reflection properties, and the decreasing-function exception for intersections."
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p50-tf-task",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": {
      "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
      "version": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "id": "p50-tf-instruction",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $g$ is the inverse function of $f$, then $f$ is the inverse function of $g$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The inverse identities $f(g(x))=x$ and $g(f(x))=x$, on their respective domains, are unchanged when the roles of $f$ and $g$ are exchanged.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If $f(g(x))=x$, then $f$ and $g$ are inverse functions of each other.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. Both compositions must be checked.",
          "worked": "False. We must also establish $g(f(x))=x$ throughout the domain of $f$. For example, $f(x)=x^2$ on $\\mathbb R$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ satisfy $f(g(x))=x$ for $x\\geq0$, but $g(f(x))=|x|$, which differs from $x$ when $x<0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$y=f(x)$ and $x=f(y)$ are reflections in $y=x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Interchanging $x$ and $y$ sends each point $(a,b)$ to $(b,a)$, its reflection in $y=x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "The points of intersection $y=f(x)$ and $y=f^{-1}(x)$ always meet on $y=x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. Decreasing functions can intersect their inverses away from $y=x$. The taught example $f(x)=-x^3$ and its inverse intersect at $(-1,1)$, $(0,0)$ and $(1,-1)$; only $(0,0)$ lies on $y=x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "If $f(a)=f(b)$ and $y=f(x)$ passes the horizontal line test, then $a=b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Passing the horizontal line test makes $f$ one-to-one, so one output cannot correspond to two distinct inputs.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "If $y=f(x)$ passes through $(3,4)$, then $y=f^{-1}(x)$ passes through $(4,3)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True, assuming the stated inverse function exists. Reflection in $y=x$ interchanges the coordinates, sending $(3,4)$ to $(4,3)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-g",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "g",
        "prompt": "If the domain of $f(x)$ is $[1,\\infty)$, then the domain of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ is $(-\\infty,1]$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The domain of $f^{-1}$ is the range of $f$, which is not specified. The given domain determines the range of $f^{-1}$ as $[1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-h",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "h",
        "prompt": "If the domain of $f(x)$ is $[1,\\infty)$, then the range of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[1,\\infty)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The range of an inverse function equals the domain of the original function, so the range of $f^{-1}$ is $[1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-tf-i",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "i",
        "prompt": "If $f$ and $g$ are inverse functions of each other, then $f(g(x))=g(f(x))$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The compositions may have different domains.",
          "worked": "False as a statement without a domain qualification. The identities $f(g(x))=x$ and $g(f(x))=x$ hold on the domains of $g$ and $f$, respectively. For example, take $f(x)=x^2$ with domain $(-\\infty,0]$ and $g(x)=-\\sqrt{x}$ with domain $[0,\\infty)$. At $x=-1$, $g(f(-1))=-1$, but $f(g(-1))$ is undefined. Wherever both compositions are defined, they do equal $x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
